WordPress needs a PHP server and a database (usually MySQL) running somewhere, plus ongoing updates and maintenance. This environment only had Node, Python, and git installed, so a static site that builds to plain HTML was the path that would actually run and be free to host. A static site is also simpler to secure and back up.

If you'd prefer WordPress, that's a reasonable choice for a blog — especially if you want to write in a visual editor, use plugins, or manage posts without touching files. It's a different setup, though: you'd typically either pay for hosting (WordPress.com, or a host like Bluehost/SiteGround) or self-host. Want me to walk you through getting a WordPress blog going instead?
